{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## The THIRD under-estimate axis: FastMCP emits the result twice (measured)\n\nThe code review on patch set 5 raised this, and it is real and material. Probed directly\nagainst the registered `CallToolRequest` handler with 2 bulky rows:\n\n```\ncontent_blocks=2  content_bytes=1020  structured_bytes=944  total/structured = 2.08x\n```\n\nA `CallToolResult` for a list tool carries **both** halves:\n\n- `structuredContent` — the whole `{\"result\": [...]}` object; and\n- one `content` text block **per row**, each serialized as `json.dumps(row, indent=2)`\n  (confirmed exactly: a single block was 510 bytes, matching `indent=2` and neither the\n  default `json.dumps` at 466 nor the compact separators at 425).\n\nSo a bound measuring only `structuredContent` passes a payload **~2.08x** the budget — the\nsame \"passes something that still overruns\" failure the other two axes cause, and the reason\nthe AC insists the measurement be the wire payload. `_wire_bytes` now counts both halves.\n\n### The regression test was a convergence twin; it no longer is\n\nThe exactness assertion previously compared `_wire_bytes(models)` against\n`json.dumps({\"result\": js_safe_result(models)})` — a second spelling of the function's own\narithmetic, so it would have passed however wrong both sides were, and it did pass while the\nmeasurement was 2x low. The oracle is now the **real emitted `CallToolResult`**, obtained by\nrunning the tool and summing every `content` block plus `structuredContent`.\n\nMutation-checked both ways: neutering the budget comparison turns 6 tests RED; reverting\n`_wire_bytes` to measure only `structuredContent` is caught by the exactness assertion\n(measured 10,862 vs 22,632 actually emitted).\n\n## `search` is the third discovery list, and it was unbounded\n\n`search`'s docstring opens *\"Search titles/descriptions/comments/tags with **bounded**\ndiscovery results\"* — but the tool had no size check at all, only the narrower\n`project_search_result` row shape making an ordinary query small. An unfiltered one was as\nunbounded as `list_tickets`. It now shares the same bound, with its own remedy naming what it\ncan actually narrow with (a more specific query, a field predicate, or its\n`status`/`ticket_type`/`has_tag` arguments) — never `ready_tickets`' `next_batch` pointer,\nwhich would be wrong for a tool that does take filters.\n"},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787970291298572535,"uuid":"a288463f-3ad3-4825-994b-db1902275e9d"}